  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard in Kibana to check how it looks.\n\n**Data:** The Kibana Sample Data Logs data view (`kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`) holds web access logs. The main fields are:\n- `response` (the HTTP status code, stored as a string)\n- `bytes`\n- `clientip`\n- `extension`\n- `geo.src` and `geo.dest`\n- `machine.os`\n- `url`, `request`, `referer` and `agent`\n- `tags`\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-6bb3f4e5 Web logs overview\" (id `569130f4-bc3c-4f81-b78c-72e58f50c2cf`) has six panels over a default range of the last 7 days:\n- Total requests\n- Average bytes\n- Unique clients\n- Requests over time\n- Requests by response code\n- Requests by extension\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-6bb3f4e5 5xx spike\" (id `ec42d4f8-4285-4931-a10e-d21dea0be825`) is an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute. It counts documents whose `response.keyword` matches `5[0-9][0-9]` over a 5-minute window. It fires when that count is above 10. The rule has no actions attached, so a firing only shows up as an alert in Kibana and doesn't notify anyone.",
